// std.blob — a lockless single-writer blob store with fenced atomic
// writes. The acceptance test of docs/design/rfc-verification.md ("Blob is
// a stdlib module"): the difficult distributed machinery is a library whose
// correctness theorems the compiler checks, not a language feature.
//
// The protocol: a BlobAuthority entity owns the data and a fencing epoch.
// A writer asks for a WriteGrant; minting a grant advances the epoch, so
// every new grant silently invalidates all older ones. A write presents
// its grant, and the authority compares the grant's token against the
// current epoch AT THE POINT OF EFFECT — a stale token is rejected with a
// typed StaleGrant before any byte lands. No locks, no client-side
// coordination: at most one writer's grant is current, so writes are
// single-writer by fencing.
//
// Each mechanism owns exactly one guarantee:
//
//   ATOMICITY  — the `object` construct: the authority's methods serialize,
//                so apply() never interleaves with grant_write() or another
//                apply(). No torn writes, by construct.
//   SAFETY     — the fence inside apply(), judged by the authority at the
//                point of effect, made a compile-time dominance proof by
//                `verify.fenced`: no write to the blob escapes it.
//   LIVENESS   — the grant: holding the newest one means your writes get
//                through. Progress, never safety.
//   DISCIPLINE — the typed StaleGrant plus mandatory handling at every
//                placement boundary: losing the race to a newer writer is
//                an inspectable outcome, not a silent no-op.
//
// The authority is an entity: share it with tasks, own it in a served
// value, and call it through a handle with `at authority { ... }` — the
// protocol is identical colocated and distributed.

// Grants and payloads cross placement boundaries as wire-shaped values.
import std.wire
// The safety theorem is carried by std.verify's named proof bundles; the
// property names become this type's exported facts.
import std.verify

// A write was refused by the fence: the presented grant's token no longer
// matches the authority's epoch. A definite failure — the authority itself
// rejected the write, so the caller KNOWS no byte landed and can react
// freely (re-request a grant, surrender, escalate). The payload says who
// out-granted you: your token vs the epoch that superseded it.
pub error StaleGrant {
    token: int
    epoch: int
}

// The write capability, as evidence: a plain value recording "at mint
// time, my token was current". Deliberately inert — no methods, no state
// machine, no validity flag: whether a grant is still current is a remote
// belief the world can falsify between any two instructions, so only the
// authority's fence may judge it. Copyable, comparable, wire-shaped.
pub class WriteGrant {
    token: int
}

// The authority — one referent, everyone shares it, every interaction is a
// serialized message. Its epoch is the fence: grants are minted FROM it
// and judged AGAINST it.
//
// The safety theorem is two named properties from std.verify, discharged
// at compile time on this body and exported by name:
//
//   verify.monotonic(self.epoch) — the epoch never decreases, proven at
//       every mut-self boundary and foreign write site. A minted token can
//       never become current again, which is what makes fencing sound.
//   verify.fenced(self.data, self.epoch, WriteGrant) — no write to `data`
//       escapes the fence: every write site must be dominated by a check
//       proving some in-scope WriteGrant's token equals the current epoch,
//       and code outside the authority may not write `data` at all.
pub object BlobAuthority satisfies verify.monotonic(self.epoch), verify.fenced(self.data, self.epoch, WriteGrant) {
    data: bytes
    epoch: int
    applied: int   // token of the last write the fence admitted

    // Protocol-specific single-state facts, statically discharged at every
    // construction and write site. The second is the interesting one: "no
    // admitted write ever carried a token newer than the newest grant" —
    // provable only because the fence is an exact match (token == epoch on
    // the fall-through path), and preserved by grant_write because
    // applied <= epoch < epoch + 1.
    invariant self.epoch >= 0
    invariant self.applied <= self.epoch

    // Mint the write capability: advance the epoch and hand back a token
    // equal to it. The increment IS the invalidation — every earlier
    // grant's token is now below the epoch, so the fence will refuse it.
    // No revocation message, no waiting on the old holder: the old write
    // *cannot* land, so nobody needs to learn whether it was attempted.
    //
    // The ensures pins the exact relation at every normal exit: a mint
    // advances the epoch by precisely one — never zero (a mint that
    // invalidated nothing), never more (token gaps). Callers assume it at
    // the call boundary without re-deriving it.
    fn grant_write(mut self) WriteGrant ensures self.epoch == old(self.epoch) + 1 {
        self.epoch = self.epoch + 1
        return WriteGrant { token: self.epoch }
    }

    // The guarded effect. Fence and write sit in one serialized method
    // body, so check-then-act is atomic by construct, and the compare
    // happens at the point of effect — never at the (possibly paused,
    // possibly partitioned) writer. A stale token raises StaleGrant before
    // any byte lands. The check is an exact match rather than "not older":
    // a forged future token bounces too, and the fall-through fact
    // tok == epoch is what discharges the applied <= epoch invariant.
    //
    // The frame ensures pins what apply must NOT do: writing never moves
    // the fence. Only grant_write mints epochs — an apply that advanced
    // (or rewound) the epoch would silently invalidate or resurrect
    // grants.
    //
    // (The store keeps raw bytes; the boundary carries string, the closest
    // wire-transferable carrier today — `bytes` cannot cross a placement
    // boundary yet.)
    fn apply(mut self, grant: WriteGrant, d: string)
        ensures self.epoch == old(self.epoch)
    {
        // The token is read into a local so the guard fact tok == epoch is
        // a named term the prover carries to the writes below.
        let tok = grant.token
        if tok != self.epoch {
            raise StaleGrant { token: tok, epoch: self.epoch }
        }
        self.applied = tok
        self.data = d.to_bytes()
    }

    // The current contents. Reads are unfenced by design: a reader sees
    // some atomically-written version (entity serialization rules out
    // torn reads), and read-freshness is a different protocol.
    fn read(self) string {
        return self.data.to_string()
    }

    // The current fence value — observability only. A caller comparing a
    // held token against this is forming a belief the world can falsify
    // before the next write; only apply()'s own compare is load-bearing.
    fn epoch_now(self) int {
        return self.epoch
    }
}

// Create an authority over an initial payload. The store starts at epoch 0
// with no admitted writes, so every pre-existing (forged) token is stale
// by construction — the first real grant is token 1.
pub fn create(initial: string) BlobAuthority {
    return BlobAuthority { data: initial.to_bytes(), epoch: 0, applied: 0 }
}
